<p>What keeps <code>ShellSubsetLarge</code> from accepting more of the corpus samples, and what to do about it. The samples: one-line commands from tldr and NL2Bash, scripts from the bash Cookbook, the pure-sh and pure-bash bibles, and ShellCheck's test strings, split into "clean" (strings ShellCheck's tests expect no warning for) and "warned" (strings its tests expect a warning for). Details: <code>claude/large-subset-ceiling.md</code>.</p>
<p><b>The yardsticks</b> (R3). <b>Upper</b>, "nothing bogus found": both shells parse the sample (<code>dash -n</code>, <code>bash --posix -n</code>), ShellCheck (<code>shellcheck -s dash</code>) gives none of its warnings SC3xxx (a construct dash lacks), and our parser did not reject it for a difference Y1 calls blatant. <b>Lower</b>, "compatible": in addition, no rejection that Y1 calls subtle. Measured 2026-11-02, after leaving out duplicates, tldr's key notation and ShellCheck's scope errors (test strings that are wrong where they stand: <code>return</code> outside a function and the like):</p>
<table class="ytab"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Samples</th><th>Accepted</th><th>Lower</th><th>Upper</th><th>Within lower, rejected</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>tldr</td><td>29,509</td><td>98.8%</td><td>99.1%</td><td>99.1%</td><td>105</td></tr>
<tr><td>NL2Bash</td><td>10,624</td><td>92.1%</td><td>93.9%</td><td>94.5%</td><td>235</td></tr>
<tr><td>bash Cookbook</td><td>81</td><td>30.9%</td><td>34.6%</td><td>35.8%</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>pure-sh-bible</td><td>44</td><td>95.5%</td><td>97.7%</td><td>97.7%</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>pure-bash-bible</td><td>122</td><td>39.3%</td><td>41.8%</td><td>41.8%</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>ShellCheck, clean</td><td>879</td><td>58.7%</td><td>61.5%</td><td>63.5%</td><td>38</td></tr>
<tr><td>ShellCheck, warned</td><td>766</td><td>57.6%</td><td>56.9%</td><td>58.1%</td><td>17</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>"Accepted" can exceed "Lower" because the subset also accepts some samples that ShellCheck warns about: it runs a program in place of dash's builtin (<code>echo</code>, decision K2 of "Shell Dialect Choices", and <code>[</code>), so dash's limits do not apply to it. The yardsticks still leave such samples out; P5 proposes counting them as excused. The 402 samples within the lower yardstick that the parser rejects are what the items Y2, Y3, P and B address; what remains after them is mostly argument forms of builtins.</p>
<p><b>Words.</b> <b>Both shells</b>: dash 0.5.12 and <code>bash --posix</code> 5.2.21; every example was run in both on 2026-11-02. <b>The parser</b>: <code>ShellSubsetLarge</code>. <b>The executor</b>: the posix_spawn session's code that runs the parsed strings. <b>Status 248</b>: what a string returns when something outside the subset shows up only at run time.</p>
